Track 1: Oxide Weeps on the Midway
- Story & Context: Recorded during the 1999 solar eclipse over an abandoned fairground outside Carrizo Plain, keyboardist Silas Vane barricaded himself inside a shuttered hall of mirrors with an ungrounded Rhodes suitcase while local marshals hammered on the plywood doors. Smelling of ozone, cheap spun sugar, and damp wool, Silas jammed a rusted butter knife between the middle-C tines, producing a weeping microtonal vibrato that made the encircling officers lower their flashlights and sway in hypnotic silence. As midday light vanished into total umbra, Judith Graves whispered the opening line into a broken calliope horn: "The sun goes blind so the marrow can breathe." The track captured the raw, accidental birth of the genre before the fairground generators sputtered out entirely.
